  <h1 class="page-title">Injury / Pain Log</h1>

  {# #620 -- report-style prose, not a bordered/background card --
       same <climbing-grade-pyramid>'s own #window-note pattern (flowing
       paragraphs, inline <strong> emphasis, no box). The mirror image of
       #597's own per-field entry-form captions: this names which field
       feeds this view. #}
  <div class="mb-5" id="view-explainer">
    <p class="text-[.95rem] text-foreground leading-[1.7]">A chronological log of climbs where you flagged pain or discomfort, plus any pattern in what's causing it.</p>
    <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted leading-[1.7] mt-2">Most recent first; each row shows the climb and the specific move(s) tagged. A ranked callout surfaces above the log once enough tags exist to spot a real pattern. Built from the <strong class="text-foreground font-semibold">Pain/injury</strong> tags on each climb — a pattern-noticing tool, not medical advice.</p>
  </div>

  {# #111 -- Performance insights are online-only, deliberately, not a
       client-side fallback computed from possibly-stale cached entries
       (Raven's own call: at the crag with poor/no connection, the
       priority is reading/updating the logbook, not reviewing aggregate
       stats -- a "review later" activity). Shown instead of the injury/pain
       log when the initial fetch fails; starts hidden, toggled in
       performance-injury-main.js's own boot(). #}
  <div class="row-card text-center max-w-[420px] mx-auto" id="performance-offline" hidden>
    <p class="text-[.9rem] text-foreground">Performance insights need a connection.</p>
    <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted mt-2">Reconnect and reload this page to see your injury/pain log.</p>
  </div>

  <div id="injury-log-root"></div>

  <h2 class="sources-heading">Sources</h2>
  <p class="text-[.82rem] text-muted leading-[1.7] mb-3">Finger flexor pulley injuries are the most common overuse injury in climbers, which is why this view exists as a pattern-spotting tool rather than a diagnostic one:</p>
  <ol class="m-0 pl-[1.2rem] text-[.84rem] leading-[1.6] text-foreground [&>li+li]:mt-[10px]">
    <li>Miro, P. H., vanSonnenberg, E., Sabb, D. M., &amp; Schöffl, V. (2021). "Finger Flexor Pulley Injuries in Rock Climbers." <em class="text-muted italic">Wilderness &amp; Environmental Medicine</em>, 32(2), 247–258. <a class="text-accent" href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33966972/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov ↗</a></li>
    <li>Jones, G., &amp; Johnson, M. I. (2016). "A Critical Review of the Incidence and Risk Factors for Finger Injuries in Rock Climbing." <em class="text-muted italic">Current Sports Medicine Reports</em>, 15(6).</li>
    <li>Jones, G., Schöffl, V., &amp; Johnson, M. I. (2018). "Incidence, Diagnosis, and Management of Injury in Sport Climbing and Bouldering: A Critical Review." <em class="text-muted italic">Current Sports Medicine Reports</em>, 17(11), 396–401. DOI: <a class="text-accent" href="https://doi.org/10.1249/JSR.0000000000000534" target="_blank" rel="noopener">10.1249/JSR.0000000000000534 ↗</a></li>
  </ol>

  <div class="text-center text-muted text-[.78rem] mt-8" id="footer"></div>
</div>
